---
import Math from './Math.astro';
import {sectors} from '../lib/quantum.mjs';
const initialSectors=sectors(3);
---
<div class="exhibit schur-lab" data-schur-lab><div class="exhibit-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Experiment 02 / Schur–Weyl decomposition</span><span class="exhibit-tag">A change of coordinates</span></div><div class="schur-intro"><div><h3>Count the coordinates in each spin sector</h3><p class="small-copy">Each cell represents one basis coordinate, not a particle. For three qubits, the two spin sectors contain four coordinates each. For four qubits, the spin-zero sector has two: these are the two basis states used in Experiment 3.</p></div><div><label class="control-label" for="schur-n">Number of qubits <output data-schur-n>3</output></label><input id="schur-n" type="range" min="2" max="8" value="3" step="1"/><div class="range-labels"><span>2</span><span>8</span></div></div></div><div class="schur-sectors" data-sectors>{initialSectors.map(s=><div class="sector"><div class="sector-label"><span>J = {Number.isInteger(s.j)?s.j:(2*s.j)+'/2'}</span><span>{s.dimension} dim.</span></div><div class="sector-art"><div class="dimension-grid" style={`grid-template-columns:repeat(${s.spinDimension},1fr);--rows:${s.multiplicity}`}>{Array.from({length:s.dimension},()=> <i/>)}</div></div><div class="sector-count">{s.spinDimension} <span>×</span> {s.multiplicity}</div><div class="young-shape" aria-label={`Young shape ${s.rows.join(', ')}`}>{s.rows.map(row=><div>{Array.from({length:row},()=> <i/>)}</div>)}</div><div class="sector-weight">{s.dimension}/8 <span>of mixed input</span></div></div>)}</div><div class="schur-total"><span data-schur-equation>4 + 4 = 8</span><span class="eyebrow">Total dimension</span></div><div class="schur-key"><p><span class="key-dot"></span><strong>Columns: spin basis states.</strong> Collective rotations act on the <Math tex={String.raw`V_J`}/> factor, with basis labels <Math tex={"m=-J,\\ldots,J"}/> and dimension <Math tex="2J+1"/>.</p><p><span class="key-dot rust"></span><strong>Rows: multiplicity.</strong> Permutations act on <Math tex={String.raw`M_J`}/>. Multiple coupling paths can reach the same total spin.</p></div><details class="exhibit-details"><summary>The decomposition, multiplicities & Young shapes</summary><div class="details-body"><Math display tex={String.raw`(\mathbb C^2)^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_J V_J\otimes M_J`}/><Math display tex={String.raw`\dim M_J=\binom n{n/2-J}-\binom n{n/2-J-1}`}/><p>The two-row Young shape is <Math tex={String.raw`(n/2+J,n/2-J)`}/>. Each rectangle above contains one cell per dimension, arranged as multiplicity × spin dimension. The small two-row diagram identifies its representation. Measuring J, sampling a Schur label, and performing a coherent Schur transform are different operations.</p><p><a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL+'#schur-weyl'}>Read the explanation of Schur–Weyl duality</a></p></div></details></div>
<script>import {initSchurLabs} from '../scripts/exhibits.mjs';initSchurLabs();</script>
